module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.CohenRing
public import Sheaf.RingTheory.FormallySmoothAdic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Rat

/-!
# Adic formal smoothness of power series over fields and Cohen rings

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Lemma Tag 07NL, parts (1) and (3)
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-power-series-cohen-formally-smooth-adic).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace MvPowerSeries

/-- Power series over a characteristic-zero field are adically formally smooth over `ℚ`.
Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Lemma Tag 07NL (1).
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-power-series-cohen-formally-smooth-adic)

Proof: Given a square-zero ideal `J` in a test `ℚ`-algebra `D` and a map `g` killing
`m ^ k`, choose lifts `dᵢ` of its variable images. Each `dᵢ ^ k` lies in `J`, so
`dᵢ ^ (2 * k) = 0`. The ideal generated by the finitely many `dᵢ` is nilpotent:
a product of `(n + 1) * (2 * k - 1) + 1` generators repeats one at least `2 * k` times.
Thus `D` and `D/J` are complete for this ideal and its image
(def-weil-pbc-adically-complete-ring). Lift the coefficient map `K → D/J` to `K → D`
by lem-weil-pbc-char-zero-field-extension-formally-smooth (Stacks Tag 0322).
The existence part of lem-weil-pbc-power-series-universal-property-complete extends
this coefficient map with the variable images `dᵢ`. Its uniqueness part in `D/J`
identifies the composite with `g`. The lift is a `ℚ`-algebra map because a ring map
from `ℚ` is unique. This is precisely def-weil-pbc-formally-smooth-adic. -/
theorem formallySmoothAdic_rat (K : Type u) [Field K] [CharZero K] (n : ℕ) :
    Algebra.FormallySmoothAdic ℚ (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) K)
      (Ideal.span (Set.range (fun i : Fin n ↦ (X i : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) K)))) := by
  sorry

/-- Power series over a Cohen ring of residue characteristic `p` are adically formally
smooth over `ℤ` for the ideal generated by `p` and the variables.
Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Lemma Tag 07NL (3).
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-power-series-cohen-formally-smooth-adic)

Proof: Given `D`, `J`, `g` and `k` as in def-weil-pbc-formally-smooth-adic, choose
lifts `dᵢ` of the variables and put `d₀ = p`. As `g` kills `m ^ k`, each of these
elements has its `k`-th power in `J` and its `2 * k`-th power zero. Their finitely
generated ideal is nilpotent by the same pigeonhole bound as in the field case.
Consequently `D` and `D/J` are complete for it and its image. The coefficient map
kills `p ^ (2 * k)` and factors through `Λ / (p ^ (2 * k))`. Apply
lem-weil-pbc-cohen-ring-mod-pn-formally-smooth (Stacks Tag 0329), using
`p ^ (2 * k) = 0` in `D`, to lift this coefficient map to `Λ → D`.
Extend it with the images `dᵢ` by lem-weil-pbc-power-series-universal-property-complete.
Uniqueness in `D/J` identifies the composite with `g`. Every ring map is a
`ℤ`-algebra map, giving the required lift and hence adic formal smoothness. -/
theorem formallySmoothAdic_int_of_isCohenRing (p : ℕ) (Λ : Type u) [CommRing Λ]
    (hΛ : IsCohenRing p Λ) (n : ℕ) :
    Algebra.FormallySmoothAdic ℤ (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) Λ)
      (Ideal.span (insert (C (p : Λ))
        (Set.range (fun i : Fin n ↦ (X i : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) Λ))))) := by
  sorry

end MvPowerSeries
